row.cell:
Bolivia, Chili and Peru war Treaty of peace between Chili and Peru;
                            protocol of, signed by Iglesias; copy of instruction No. 8, to Mr.
                            Phelps, on the subiect, inclosed.

Corea and its relations with other nations: Conservative spirit of the
                            people opposed to; liberal sentiments of the King in favor of;
                            proclamation attributed to him, inclosed.

Emigration of China: Not practicable for China as a relief from its
                            over-population, because of Chinese unfithess for colonization;
                            reopening of Portuguese nort of Macao to emigration.

Death of President Zaldua: His rare attainments and fine abilities;
                            reputation as a great political leader appointment ofof a new
                            cabinet.

Location of legation in Corea: Selection of site of; description of
                            the, habitation; members of household. Extremes of heat and cold;
                            markets, &c.

American pork: Prohibition of importation of; copy of petition to
                            minister of commerce by French importers, relative to, received;
                            regarded as indicating tendency of sound opinion in France.

Death of Senator Laboulaye: Among his deserved titles was his right to
                            the gratitude of the American people as the devoted friend of America;
                            large and distinguished attendance at his funeral.

Submarine cables: International conference for the protection of;
                            co-operation of the United States desired upon basis of memorandum
                            submitted: designation of delegates requested.

Reorganization of justice in Tunis under the protectorate of France;
                            requests of United States the renunciation of right of
                            extraterritoriality of its citizens in Tunis.

Submarine cables: International conference for the protection of;
                            protocol for convention to be submitted to the different powers;
                            requests adhesion of United States as promptly as may be.

Submarine cables: International conference for the protection of;
                            reassembling of, October 15, 1883, proposed; assent to, of the United
                            States requested.

Submarine cables: International conference for the protection of;
                            project of a convention for; delegates of the United States have been
                            instructed to join in.

Submarine cables: International conference for the protection of;
                            delay of three months in signing the final agreement, within which time
                            amendment may be submitted; copies of the proceedings inclosed.

Submarine cables: International conference for the protection of;
                            United States is prepared to sign convention for, on the day
                            fixed.

American pork: Proposed prohibitory legislation against the
                            importation of; the discussion of, in the Reichstag as a hopeful sign;
                            proposition for an expert commission from Germany approved.

American pork: Prohibition of the importation of; approval of decree
                            by Bundesraht; minister requests answer to President’s invitation;
                            correspondence inclosed.

American pork: Expert commission for examination of question;
                            invitation of United States not replied to action of Bundesrath
                            adverse.

Naturalized Americans: Liability of, to military service in Germany;
                            questions arising in and principles involved are practically settled;
                            report of cases arising and disposed of during the year ending October,
                            1883. inclosed.

American pork in Germany: Trichinosis, origin, extent, and spread of;
                            report on, by Consul Fox demonstrates that American pork is exonerated
                            from charge of being cause of; that the inspection laws of Germany are
                            insufficient; and all disease is due to eating pork not properly cooked;
                            report inclosed.

American pork in Germany: Epidemic of trichinosis in Eumesleben;
                            progress and condition of; contributions made for the relief of the
                            sufferers.

Arrest of American citizens: Cases of Edmund O’Brien Kennedy (alias Featherstone), and Thomas Gallagher;
                            investigation into causes of their arrest; correspondence relating
                            thereto.

International Fisheries Exhibition: Prospective success of; great
                            credit due to the United States and Smithsonian Institution therefor;
                            excellence of the display made by the United States.

Trial of Patrick O’Donnell: Interview with accused; claims
                            naturalization in 1876, after seven years’ residence in United States;
                            refers to Michael McGinley as withess to his naturalization.

Defense of Patrick O’Donnell: Father of accused was Michael O’Donnell;
                            dead eighteen years, and never a naturalized American, and when in
                            America lived in Pennsylvania.

International Fisheries Exhibition: Co-operation in, of the United
                            States; special expression of executive committee of gratitude for;
                            commendatory mention of Professor Goode.

Incursions of Indians across the Northwestern boundary: Bands of
                            Canadian Indians in Montana Territory; collision with troops imminent;
                            copy of telegram inclosed.

Chinese immigration act of the United States: The provisions do not
                            apply to natives of Hong-Kong merely passing through United States;
                            extract from opinion of Attorney-General quoted.

Incursions by British Indians into Montana Territory: Horses stolen
                            from Americans by Crees; telegram relative to from War Department,
                            inclosed.

Indian raids across the northwestern boundary: Understanding between
                            commanding officers of the two Governments for the suppression of;
                            copies of correspondence in regard to inclosed.

American pork: Prohibition of the importation of, insisted on by
                            Greece; action justified because of the prohibitory measures of France;
                            correspondence with the foreign office inclosed.

Chinese immigration to Hawaii: Cabinet resolution against, rescinded;
                            Chinese laborers to be admitted in numbers not to exceed 600 in any
                            three months; correspondence relative to, inclosed.

Rebellion in Hayti: Capitulation attempted by the diplomatic corps;
                            participation in, by Mr. Langston cannot be deemed as authorized by the
                            united States.

Rebellion in Hayti: Increase and spread of, throughout the Republic;
                            district of Jacmel in arms against the Government; it and districts of
                            Port-au-Prince and Léogane in state of siege; port of Jacmel blockaded;
                            conference of diplomatic corps with President at his request; complaint
                            against British and American consular officers.

Corea and Japan, treaty of friendship and commerce between: Report
                            that Japan has remitted the $550,000 indemnity Corea promised to pay;
                            copy of treaty inclosed.

Political: Mexican Congress; chief features of President’s message;
                            public improvements and finance; while in the railroad and telegraph
                            enterprises there appears to have been progress, the financial outlook
                            for the Government is not so favorable; subsidies for railroad,
                            telegraph, and immigration contracts, added to expense of a large
                            standing army, have caused unusual expenditures.

Indian incursions across the border: Measures for the prevention of;
                            copy of plan proposed to Mexican Government by Senate and
                            Representatives of Mexico inclosed.

Indian raids across the border: Measures for the prevention of;
                            provisions in each Republic for the disarming of Indians; prevention of
                            sale by them of boot would be a salutary plan.

Indian incursions across the border: Agreement for prevention of;
                            objection to plan restoring to owners booty in possession of raiders
                            when captured reported to Mexican Government.

Alexander III, coronation of: Description of the ceremonies of;
                            participation in by Mr. Hunt and Admiral Baldwin, United States Navy;
                            correspondence relative to, inclosed.

The French in Tonquin: Arrival of French troops at Haiphong;
                            withdrawal of Chinese forces from Tonquin; difficulties on the Song-Koi;
                            extract from Hong-Kong journal.

Trade with Cuba: Extracts from budget recently adopted for Cuba;
                            attention called to part relating to American commerce; appointment of
                            Ignacio Maria del Castillo, governor general of Cuba.

Petroleum storage concession: Minister of interior’s promise of
                            modification of, to the statu quo ante;
                            this change due to legation’s direct recourse to the Sultan.

Treaty right of Americans in Turkey: Deprivation of, in the arrest,
                            imprisonment of, and assault upon Dr. Pflaum by local authorities;
                            demand for damages for violation of Article IV of treaty of 1830;
                            question of, transferred to imperial ministry of justice.

Outrage on Drs. Knapp and Raynolds: Delay in obtaining redress;
                            courteous action of Lord Dufferin and British consul at Erzroum;
                            correspondence relative to, inclosed.

Claims of United States against Venezuela: Suspension of payment on;
                            copy of protest of United States against, delivered to minister of
                            foreign affairs.

Claims of the United States against Venezuela: Suspension of payments
                            of; reply to United States’ request for resumption of payments;
                            Venezuela does not disavow convention of 1866; fraud, tainted awards
                            based on it; payments made go further than justice demands; abides by
                            its decision of depositing the credits in Caracos instead of Washington;
                            copy of note of Mr. Seyas inclosed.
